<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Aka

//aka// — Hawaiian: image, reflection, likeness; to be like. The English //also-known-as// happens to
share the same three letters, and it happens to share LIKENESS too — a happy accident this root leans
on, never a borrowing.

''One family, two axes.'' `aka` and `kahea` (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kahea`) sugar the SAME
`pranala family=transclusion` — one edge family, split on PROPAGATION (`kahea` push-forward · `aka`
none/pinned) crossed with SCOPE (a head-position carrier-scope declaration · a body embed). `aka`
holds the pinned pole of propagation at both scopes; the scope axis decides what the pin DOES.

''Head position: a pinned likeness.'' Standing at a carrier's own head, `aka` declares the carrier's
likeness to — conformance with — the target, a carrier-scope declaration rather than an embed: the
carrier reads AS LIKE its target without inlining the target's bytes. Most of the corpus actually
reaches for `aka` here: 218 of 249 canon uses sit in head position, and 188 of those declare
RFC-2119's normative language.

''Body position: a pinned frozen image.'' Inside a carrier's body, `aka` embeds a frozen image of the
target instead — its content pinned at the moment the embed resolves, read-only, no ownership stake,
the target's bytes inlining at the source position.

A TW5 shadow tiddler names something else entirely, and the resemblance misleads: a shadow tiddler
names a LIVE fallback layer, visible only while nothing overrides it — the opposite posture from a
frozen pin, which never yields to an override and never falls back. `aka` pins once, at the moment the
embed or declaration resolves, and holds that pin until the carrier re-weaves.

Sugar for `<<~ pranala from=? -> to=URI family=transclusion>>`. The `transclusion` family is confidence-bounded:
a frozen embed can age from its source, so edges SHOULD carry a confidence rating when the embed is uncertain.

''English weave name: `snapshot`.'' `aka` weaves in English as `snapshot`, not as a mirror of any
other sigil's weave — `snapshot` names its own frozen-image posture directly, the way the target
reads at the moment the weave pins it. `shadow` rides beside it as a read-only mirror only
(`lar-mirror-of: aka`), held back from carrying `snapshot`'s weave slot because TiddlyWiki already
spends "shadow tiddler" on plugin-default semantics; minting `shadow` as the primary weave would
collide with that existing TW5 sense.

''Live/frozen split.'' `aka` and `kahea` (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kahea`) sugar the same
`transclusion` family from opposite propagation postures, and each links the other directly. `kahea`
weaves ''live'' — as an include, re-resolving the target on every render pass. `aka` weaves
''frozen'' — by inlining the target's content pinned at the moment the weave runs, so the source
carries a snapshot rather than a subscription. Neither sigil mirrors the other: `aka` names its own
sigil, its own family-contract row (`propagation:none` against `kahea`'s `propagation:push-forward`),
and its own English weave name.

''Weave slice, built.'' The projection render pass emits "frozen `aka` references" at the
wiki→snapshot boundary (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/render-pipeline#/three-render-modes`); the
general inline-weave slice that stands `aka` up as a first-class content-substitution pass, distinct
from that projection-render special case, stands built — `weaveAka` (weave/index.ts) inlines the
target's current text, pinned with the target's own `ni:` check, whole-carrier or `#/slot`-scoped.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/ooda-ha>>

✶ sense the target URI — this meme observes that one; note whether confidence bound is needed
⏿ orient: family:transclusion, traversal:source-to-target, propagation:none; read-only; no execution pulse
◇ confidence-bounded: if the observation carries uncertainty, SHOULD declare confidence on the edge
▶ emit EdgeSugarNode with sigil:aka, family:transclusion; render the pinned likeness/frozen image at source position
↺ target content is live-readable at source; source holds no lifecycle stake in target; confirm the pinned image stable; target still resolvable; no ownership or mutation transferred

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! Law (Kānāwai)

An aka MUST bind a source socket and a target URI.
An aka MUST carry `family:transclusion`.
An aka MUST leave the target unchanged.
An aka MUST stay inert, carrying no execution pulse.
An aka SHOULD carry `confidence` when the observation is uncertain.
An aka MUST stay acyclic — directly and transitively.

`family:transclusion` — frozen content embedding; the source shows the target's content through
without consuming or owning it. Propagation is none: a frozen embed is pulled, not pushed.

The `transclusion` family is `confidence-bounded: true`. This means the parser flags aka edges
that lack a confidence rating when the source-side context signals uncertainty.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/syntax>>

!! Syntax

```text
<<~ aka URI>>
<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/meme">>
<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language">>
```

`?` source resolves to the nearest enclosing socket.
Fragment `#slot-name` addresses a named ahu child directly.

Regex (canonical):
```
/<<~\s*aka\s+(\S+)\s*>>/
```
Groups: `[full, target-uri]`

Full pranala expansion:
```text
<<~ pranala from="?" -> to="URI" family="transclusion" lifecycle="instance" traversal="source-to-target" propagation="none">>
```

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/schema>>

!! Schema (machine-readable)

Canonical TOML form. Source of truth for `BUILTIN_AKA_RE` and `akaDefaultFamily`
in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/pranala-parser.ts`.

```toml
sigil          = "aka"
kind           = "edge-sugar"
layer          = "both"
default-family = "transclusion"
render-mode    = null
alias          = []

pattern = '<<~\s*aka\s+(\S+)\s*>>'

[captures]
to = 1

[family-contract]
role-recommended   = false
confidence-bounded = true
```

